Committed. Here's what happened.

## What I built

tests/cargo_spawning_tests.rs — the seventh deterministic gate, same shape as its six siblings: pure table-tested classifier, all filesystem walking at one call site, fatal on the unnamed case, debt register, ratchet, limits printed through a raw stderr() handle.

The predicate is two-level, one hop, and the spawner set is derived, never hand-written — strictly better than GLOBAL_SETTERS, which needs a second test to tie it to reality. A rename here moves both halves at once.

## The measurement, which was the deliverable

94 files, 7,276 `fn`s, 5,220 `#[test]`s scanned; 2 spawners derived; 8 tests reach one; 8 registered.

#834's own body predicted **three** register entries, from #832's `grep` sweep. The scan returned **eight** — a `grep` for a function name in a 1,700-line file undercounts its callers. That gap *is* the finding, and it's exactly why the register was generated by running the scan and pasting what it found rather than by curating it.

Nothing was fixed, on purpose. No src/ file touched. Every entry states what's true and no more: cargo audit --version is a subcommand probe, it builds nothing and cannot clobber `target/debug/yoyo, so it is *not* #832's defect — but its answer still depends on the machine's toolchain, and security_audit_command_rust` accommodates both `Ok` and `Err`, so it asserts nothing about the Rust arm in particular.

## Positive controls — and one of them nearly lied to me

All four run, not assumed. Delete a register entry → fatal with the pasteable remedy naming that file and test. Bogus entry → fatal on the ratchet. Neuter the derivation to return empty → fatal on the anti-vacuous branch. Real counts non-zero.

But my first attempt issued two file-mutating controls in one parallel tool block, and they raced on the same file: one printed nothing, the other passed when it should have failed. A false all-clear on a control whose entire job is to prove the gate can fail. Re-run serially, both fired. That's the same class this gate enumerates, one layer up, in the act of verifying it — so it went into CLAUDE.md rather than into my head.

## Also

• Filed **#835** for extracting the shared brace scanner (the second copy is deliberate and the module doc says so; a reader surface is not a scheduler surface).
• Corrected the now-stale commands_info.rs clause claiming "nothing enumerates the rest of the tree" — recorded as superseded rather than erased, with the honest split: the enumeration exists, the repair doesn't, so #834 stays open on its option 1.

